| 1 | 3 | Art detectives in the studio. *How can clues in a work tell us how it was made and what it says, and how can printing, collage and clay come together in one work?* | 18 | va-g3-look; va-g3-craft | VA:Re7.1.3a; VA:Re7.2.3a; VA:Cr2.1.3a; VA:Cr2.2.3a | Responding (Re7); creating (Cr2); connecting touched through each work's setting card. Works: Romare Bearden, The Block (United States, 1971, a six-panel collage of a Harlem street, the Metropolitan Museum of Art), described and linked, never copied; an Asante adinkra cloth (Ghana, stamped with carved calabash stamps; the unit plan picks a cloth from a museum's open-access page, such as the Smithsonian National Museum of African Art's), studied for how its stamps were made and what its symbols mean; Elizabeth Catlett, Sharecropper (a linocut made in Mexico, 1952, an impression at the Art Institute of Chicago), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: sorting pictures by feeling (va-g2-look) and the clean-up card (va-g2-craft). VA:Re7.1.3a: find clues in a work (edges, layers, repeated marks, tool marks) and guess how the artist made it, then check the guess on the work card. VA:Re7.2.3a: work out the message a picture sends, with a "because" pointing to the detail that sends it. VA:Cr2.1.3a: make a finished work the child is happy with that joins at least two processes: a foam-plate print (lines pressed with a pencil, water-based ink rolled with a brayer), a torn and cut collage layer, and a small modeled clay or dough piece mounted beside it. VA:Cr2.2.3a: show safe, skilled handling in each process (the brayer and ink tray, scissors and glue, the clay tools and wet clay kept covered), aloud and in action. Words: clue, process, print, collage, modeling, message. Showing: the finished panels as a gallery for a partner class, each with a one-line process label. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Re7.1.3a, a written item: two clues on an unseen print or collage and a guess at how it was made; VA:Re7.2.3a, a written item: the message of the same unseen work, with the detail that sends it; VA:Cr2.1.3a, a guide look-for on the finished panel: two or more processes joined, with the child's one-line note on why they are happy with it; VA:Cr2.2.3a, a table item: with five at the table, each child shows the safe, skilled use of one tool from the tray (brayer, scissors or clay tool) and says one rule. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: inventing a technique of one's own (grade 4). Tools: step cards for printing, collage and modeling, a clue card, work cards, Curio Class's own; a work viewer on the teacher screen or the printed card. Free path: foam trays from packaging, washable paint for ink, magazine paper, flour-salt dough. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | First because every later unit of the year needs the studio routine and the looking routine at grade 3's step (the safe-studio and look-describe-interpret models come back here, retrieved where first needed). Guessing a process from clues comes before the child's own printing and collage, so the looking teaches what to try. Longest grade 3 unit: four ids, three processes in one finished work and the year's studio set-up | Candidate: the bar's criterion 9 on set-up and clean-up minutes in a 40-minute printing session with 30 children | Hazards and controls: printing ink and paint on hands and tables (smocks or old shirts, covered tables, hands washed); brayers and ink trays handed out by monitors, never shared mid-roll; blunt-tip scissors only in year 3; clay kept moist, not eaten, wheat allergy checked (moist clay instead of flour dough); foam trays washed, no meat trays. Adinkra cloth was first a mourning cloth: the unit tells it plainly, studies the symbols by looking, and no child copies adinkra symbols (each child designs their own stamp mark); the sensitivity agent reads the holding museum's page and an Asante or Ghanaian source on adinkra first. Catlett's print shows a Black woman farm worker; the unit tells its setting (Catlett's prints of working people, made in Mexico) and the sensitivity agent reads the museum's page first. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 2 | 3 | Growing an idea. *How do artists make an invented idea richer, and how does a partner's question help us add what a work needs?* | 16 | va-g3-ideas; va-g3-revise | VA:Cr1.1.3a; VA:Cr1.2.3a; VA:Cr3.1.3a | Creating (Cr1, Cr3); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Henri Rousseau, The Repast of the Lion (France, about 1907, the Metropolitan Museum of Art, open access), a jungle he never saw, built from imagination and from the plants and pictures he studied; a page from the Hamzanama (Mughal India, about 1562 to 1577, painted in the emperor's workshop for a long adventure story; the unit plan picks a page without battle from the Brooklyn Museum's or the Metropolitan Museum of Art's open-access pages); the invented worlds of Shaun Tan (Australia, born 1974; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked to the artist's own page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: many solutions and a question of the child's own (va-g2-ideas) and the two-way talk (va-g2-revise). VA:Cr1.1.3a: take a made-up idea (an invented creature, place or machine) and add detail and depth over three idea pages: a foreground, a middle and a background, and details of texture and pattern. VA:Cr1.2.3a: choose and use the resources, tools and technology on hand to pursue the idea (a mirror for a face, a magnifier for texture, a viewfinder, reference picture cards, or a drawing tool on a shared device in turns) and say what each helped with. VA:Cr3.1.3a: after a short partner talk (describe, ask, suggest), add detail that strengthens the meaning that is appearing, and write one line on what was added and why. Technique: overlapping for depth, and layering colored pencil or oil pastel. Words: imagine, detail, depth, foreground, background, strengthen. Showing: the invented worlds with their idea pages beside them, for the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Cr1.1.3a, a guide look-for on the idea pages: the first idea and at least two added layers of detail or depth; VA:Cr1.2.3a, a kept card made on the last making day: the two tools or resources the child chose and what each helped them do; VA:Cr3.1.3a, a guide look-for on the revised work with its one-line note: a detail added after the partner talk that makes the meaning clearer. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: brainstorming routes with a group (grade 4). Tools: idea-page step cards, the "tools I chose" card, a partner talk card, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencils, scrap paper, a hand mirror. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 1 because adding depth uses the clue-reading of how works are layered, and the year's studio is set; before unit 3 because a map and an observed drawing need the habit of adding detail and the partner talk that decides what to add. The partner talk practices ELA grade 3's collaborative talk (Cross-subject) | Candidate: a fourth invented world by a living maker from Africa or Latin America, described and linked | Hamzanama pages often show battles and demons: the unit plan picks a page without violence, and the sensitivity agent reads the museum's page first. Partner talk: a child who finds talk hard may point and use the talk card's frames. Oil pastel and colored pencil labeled non-toxic; hands washed. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 3 | 3 | Maps, models and mood. *How can a map or model show a place we know, and how do an artist's materials make a mood we can judge?* | 14 | va-g3-design; va-g3-judge | VA:Cr2.3.3a; VA:Cn10.1.3a; VA:Re8.1.3a; VA:Re9.1.3a | Creating (Cr2); connecting (Cn10); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: a stick chart from the Marshall Islands (Micronesia, nineteenth or twentieth century, sticks and shells mapping ocean swells and islands for navigators; the unit plan picks one on a holding museum's page), described and linked; Gustave Caillebotte, Paris Street; Rainy Day (France, 1877, the Art Institute of Chicago, open access), a built place and a mood made with paint | Retrieval in day 2's opener: an old object given a new use (va-g2-design) and meaning from mood, subject and form (va-g2-judge). VA:Cr2.3.3a: alone or in a group, make a map, diagram or model of a familiar place (the classroom, the playground or the route to the library), with a key of three symbols, and a box-and-paper model of one part. VA:Cn10.1.3a: make a drawing from what the child observes on a walk around the school, with three details that are really there. VA:Re8.1.3a: work out what a work means by studying how its media (paint, sticks, ink) build its subject, its form and its mood. VA:Re9.1.3a: judge a work with the teacher's three-line checklist, one reason for each line (the standards-set model begins). Words: map, key, symbol, model, mood, checklist. Showing: the maps and models as a "know our school" table for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: VA:Cr2.3.3a, a guide look-for on the map or model: the place recognizable, a key with three symbols, parts in the order they really sit; VA:Cn10.1.3a, a guide look-for on the observed drawing: three details seen on the walk; VA:Re8.1.3a, a written item: how the media of an unseen work build its subject and mood, and what it means; VA:Re9.1.3a, a written item: the same work judged with a new three-line checklist, a reason for each line. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: measured scale (bigger things are drawn bigger, in the order they sit; no ratio or measuring, lesson 13); built places of the region (grade 4). Tools: the "made for" card, map step cards, the checklist card, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: cereal boxes, scrap paper, pencils. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 2 because a map and an observed drawing build on adding detail; judging with a checklist needs unit 1's clue-reading and adds media and mood. Before unit 4 so the year's show has maps, models and invented worlds to choose from. The standards-set model starts here | Candidate: a third place-and-mood work by a living maker from Asia or Africa, described and linked | Hazards and controls: the school walk stays on school grounds, the class together and in sight; cardboard cut with a blade is cut by an adult; glue sticks and tape. Maps show the school only, never a child's home or route home. Stick charts carry navigators' knowledge: the sensitivity agent reads the holding museum's page and a Marshallese source on navigation first; no child is asked to copy one. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 4 | 3 | Stories and history in art. *How have people used art to tell stories and keep history, and how does knowing when and where a work was made change what we think of it?* | 12 | va-g3-show; va-g3-museum | VA:Pr4.1.3a; VA:Pr5.1.3a; VA:Pr6.1.3a; VA:Cn11.1.3a | Presenting (Pr4, Pr5, Pr6); connecting (Cn11); responding practiced on each work (review). Works: Jacob Lawrence, The Migration Series (United States, 1940 to 1941, 60 panels shared by the Phillips Collection and the Museum of Modern Art), chosen panels described and linked; a Lakota winter count (pictures painted on hide or cloth, one for each year, keeping a band's history; the unit plan picks one on the Smithsonian's pages), described and linked; the Bayeux embroidery (Normandy, about 1070, wool on linen telling the Norman conquest of England, the Bayeux Museum), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: a theme exhibit (va-g2-show) and two cultures on work cards (va-g2-museum). VA:Pr4.1.3a: walk the classroom, the hallway and a paper mock-up of the class web page as show spaces, and list what each can and cannot do for a work. VA:Pr5.1.3a: choose one space, mount the work and write a short artist note (title, process, one sentence of meaning). VA:Pr6.1.3a: describe how and where three cultures told stories and kept history in art (panels in a series, a picture for each year, a long embroidery). VA:Cn11.1.3a: write a first reaction to a work, read its setting card, and say how the reaction changed. Words: story, history, record, setting, space, artist note. Showing: the year-end class show in the chosen spaces, for families, with the family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Pr4.1.3a, a kept card finished on the unit's last making day: the three spaces, one "can" and one "cannot" each; VA:Pr5.1.3a, a guide look-for on the showing: the chosen space, the mounted work and its artist note; VA:Pr6.1.3a, a written item: how and where one of the three cultures recorded a story or history; VA:Cn11.1.3a, a written item: a first reaction to an unseen work, then the reaction after its setting card, and what changed. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: technology for keeping art (grade 4). Tools: the show-space card, artist-note card, work and setting cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: cereal-box mounts. Once-a-week path: 5 sessions | Last in year 3 because the show is chosen from the year's kept work (units 1 to 3) and ends the year after the year-end check (week 26); the unit's showing in weeks 27 to 32 is its own assessment. The exhibit-plan and work-card models move to grade 3's step here | Candidate: a story-telling work from Asia or Africa beside the three, by a living maker | Hard history: the Great Migration was a flight from violence and unfair laws; the unit picks panels of travel, work and new homes, and leaves out the panels that show violence; the winter count may record war, sickness or hunger years, named in one plain sentence; the Bayeux embroidery shows a battle, shown in the panels of building, sailing and feasting. The sensitivity agent reads the Phillips Collection's pages on the series, the Smithsonian's pages on winter counts with Lakota keepers' own words, and the Bayeux Museum's page first. The class web page is the school's own and only the teacher posts to it, under each child's chosen name with the family's yes. Glue sticks; hands washed. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 5 | 4 | Marks and messages. *How does making a piece like an artist's change what we see in theirs, and which parts of a picture carry its message?* | 16 | va-g4-look; va-g4-craft | VA:Re7.1.4a; VA:Re7.2.4a; VA:Cr2.1.4a; VA:Cr2.2.4a | Responding (Re7); creating (Cr2). Works: Vincent van Gogh, Wheat Field with Cypresses (the Netherlands-born artist in France, 1889, the Metropolitan Museum of Art, open access), marks that move; an ink drawing by Ibrahim El-Salahi (born 1930 in Sudan; the unit plan picks a work on the Tate's or another museum's page and records whether living, as of its date), described and linked; a poster from the Library of Congress's collection of posters made by the national works program of 1936 to 1943 (public domain; the unit plan picks a parks or library poster), a picture built to send one message | Retrieval in day 2's opener: clues to how a work was made (va-g3-look) and safe, skilled handling (va-g3-craft). VA:Re7.1.4a: write a first reaction to a work, make a small piece in similar media (marks in ink or watered paint), write a second reaction, and compare the two. VA:Re7.2.4a: mark the parts of an image that deliver its message (the biggest shape, the words, the colors, where the eye goes first) and say what each part does. VA:Cr2.1.4a: test a set of mark-making tools on a sample sheet (pencil, brush, stick, sponge, finger) and invent and name one technique of the child's own, with its steps. VA:Cr2.2.4a: handle supplies, tools and equipment with care so no one gets hurt, and pass the tool check before using pointed scissors or other pointed tools (carry, use, pass and store, and why each step keeps others safe). The practice-log model starts: each technique is a dated entry in the sketchbook. Words: mark, technique, invent, reaction, message, care. Showing: the sample sheets and the invented-technique cards as a "technique library" for the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Re7.1.4a, a kept card made on the last making days: the first reaction, the small piece, the second reaction and the change named; VA:Re7.2.4a, a written item: two parts of an unseen poster that carry its message and what each does; VA:Cr2.1.4a, a guide look-for on the sample sheet: five tools tested and one invented technique named with its steps; VA:Cr2.2.4a, a table item: each child at the table shows the safe carry, use, pass and storage of pointed scissors and says how it keeps others from harm. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: skill built over a long practice log (grade 5). Tools: a mark sampler tool or its printed sample sheet, step cards, the tool-check card, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: sticks, sponges, watered paint, scrap paper. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in year 4 because it reopens the studio with the tool check before any pointed tool and turns grade 3's clues into named parts that carry a message. Shorter than year 3's unit 1 (16 days): one technique strand, not three processes, so year 4's museum unit can have the days its five ids need. The practice-log model starts here, where it is first needed | Candidate: a mark-making work by a living Native American maker beside the three | Hazards and controls: ink is washable or watered paint (no permanent ink); smocks, covered tables, hands washed; pointed scissors only after the child passes the tool check, carried closed and point down, stored in a closed box; sticks blunt. The national works program poster is government art: the unit tells its setting (jobs for artists in the 1930s). El-Salahi's work draws on Arabic script and Sudanese settings: the sensitivity agent reads the museum's page and the artist's own published words first, and no child copies script as decoration. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 6 | 4 | Making it together. *How does a group of artists set a goal that matters to them, and how does a critique help a work in progress?* | 16 | va-g4-ideas; va-g4-revise | VA:Cr1.1.4a; VA:Cr1.2.4a; VA:Cr3.1.4a | Creating (Cr1, Cr3); responding practiced on the murals (review). Works: Judy Baca and the young people who painted The Great Wall of Los Angeles (United States, begun 1976, a half-mile mural of California history in a flood channel; the artist's center's own pages; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked; the murals of Chicano Park, San Diego (begun 1973 by community artists under a freeway, on the park's own pages), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: adding detail and depth (va-g3-ideas) and the partner talk (va-g3-revise). VA:Cr1.1.4a: brainstorm at least three different approaches to one design problem (a panel that welcomes new students to the school) before the group chooses. VA:Cr1.2.4a: set goals as a group (what the panel must say, who it is for, each member's part) and make a work that matters to its makers. VA:Cr3.1.4a: change the panel in progress using ideas from a small-group critique with set steps and roles (speaker, listener, recorder; describe, ask, suggest; the artist answers with one planned change). The critique-protocol model starts. Technique: a group paper mural panel with a shared color plan and a grid to join each member's section. Words: brainstorm, approach, goal, group, critique, revise. Showing: the welcome panels hung in the entrance hall for the school. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Cr1.1.4a, a guide look-for on the sketchbook: three approaches that differ, sketched by the child; VA:Cr1.2.4a, a kept card: the group's goal card (goal, why it matters to us, each member's part) and a look-for on the panel: the child's section meets the goal; VA:Cr3.1.4a, a guide look-for on the revised section with the recorder's sheet: one change made from the critique, with the comment it came from. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet; each child is marked on their own sketches, card line and section. Out: merging two ideas (grade 5). Tools: the brainstorm sheet, the goal card, the critique-protocol card with roles, Curio Class's own. Free path: kraft or newsprint roll, two or three colors of paint or crayon. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 5 because the panel uses the year's tested and invented marks and the tool care; the critique-protocol model starts here, before unit 7's judging and every later critique. Before the mid-year check (week 17) so a group product is finished before the check samples it | Candidate: a group-made work from Asia or Africa beside the two murals | Group work: each child's mark rests on their own sketches, their line of the goal card and their own section, never on the group's whole panel. "Matters to its makers" is the school, not a child's home; a child may draw a public or invented welcome scene. The Great Wall of Los Angeles tells hard parts of California history: the unit names its making with young people and shows sections the sensitivity agent passes; Chicano Park's murals grew from a community's claim to a park: the agent reads the park's own pages and SPARC's (the artist's center) first. Hazards and controls: paint on hands and floor (drop cloths, covered tables, hands washed); the roll laid on tables, never on a stair or a walkway. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 7 | 4 | Built places, two cities. *How do artists record the buildings of a place, and how can one checklist judge two works fairly?* | 14 | va-g4-design; va-g4-judge | VA:Cr2.3.4a; VA:Re8.1.4a; VA:Re9.1.4a | Creating (Cr2); responding (Re8, Re9); connecting touched through each city's setting card. Works: Canaletto, Piazza San Marco (Venice, late 1720s, the Metropolitan Museum of Art, open access); Zhang Zeduan, Along the River During the Qingming Festival (China, early twelfth century, a handscroll of a city's streets, bridges and boats, the Palace Museum, Beijing), described and linked; the region's own buildings, photographed by the teacher and credited | Retrieval in day 2's opener: maps and models of familiar places (va-g3-design) and judging with a given checklist (va-g3-judge). VA:Cr2.3.4a: record, describe and depict built places in the region (a building near the school, or the teacher's photographs of the town's buildings): a drawing from looking, with overlap and a horizon line, and a three-sentence written description of what was recorded. VA:Re8.1.4a: read a short background card on a work, then study its subject, form and media to say what it means. VA:Re9.1.4a: use one checklist on two works and give a reason for each line on each. Words: record, depict, region, background, form, media. Showing: a "buildings of our region" wall for another class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Cr2.3.4a, a guide look-for on the drawing and its description: a real regional building drawn from looking, with three features described; VA:Re8.1.4a, a written item: the meaning of an unseen work from its background card, subject, form and media; VA:Re9.1.4a, a written item: one checklist used on two unseen works, a reason for each line on each. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: standards that shift with culture and style (grade 5). Tools: the "made for" card, a building-drawing step card, background cards, the checklist card, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencils, clipboards of card, the teacher's printed photographs. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 6 because judging two works uses the critique protocol's describe, ask and suggest; one product, three ids, so 14 days. Before unit 8 because inferring a work's time and place from clues grows from reading background cards here | Candidate: a third city view by a maker from Africa or Latin America, described and linked | Hazards and controls: drawing outside stays on school grounds or on the sidewalk in view, with a second adult for any walk off the grounds; the class stays together. A building near a child's home is never asked for; the region's public buildings are the set. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 8 | 4 | Keeping art, sharing traditions. *How have people kept and shown art across time and places, and how can our art show a tradition of our community?* | 14 | va-g4-show; va-g4-museum | VA:Pr4.1.4a; VA:Pr5.1.4a; VA:Pr6.1.4a; VA:Cn11.1.4a; VA:Cn10.1.4a | Presenting (Pr4, Pr5, Pr6); connecting (Cn10, Cn11). Works: the cave temples of Mogao, Dunhuang (China, painted from the fourth to the fourteenth century, a World Heritage site, kept today by limiting visitors and by digital records), studied by looking only; a woven tapestry by D.Y. Begay (DinAc, a weaver of the Navajo Nation; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked to the holding museum's or the artist's page; a set of unseen objects on Curio Class cards from open-access pages, for inferring setting; a museum, a gallery and a community center near the school, from their own pages or a visit | Retrieval in day 2's opener: a chosen space and an artist note (va-g3-show) and how setting changes a reaction (va-g3-museum). VA:Pr4.1.4a: analyze how past, present and emerging technologies changed keeping and showing art (glass cases and low light, photographs, climate control, digital scans and 3D copies), on setting cards about Mogao. VA:Pr5.1.4a: analyze what matters when showing and guarding art indoors and outdoors, for a short time or for good, and on paper or on a screen, then plan how the class's work is shown and protected in two places (a wall and a printed or screen slide set). VA:Pr6.1.4a: compare what a museum, a gallery and a community center aim to do and the experiences they offer visitors. VA:Cn11.1.4a: look closely at an object and infer from clues when, where and in what culture it was made. VA:Cn10.1.4a: make a piece that shows a tradition of the child's community (a festival, a market, a parade, a local craft), taken from a community source (a local cultural center's page, the library's local-history pages, a guest the school invites), with a public or invented tradition marked the same; an application day, scored as making. Words: preserve, technology, format, venue, infer, tradition. Showing: the tradition pieces shown on the wall and in the slide set for families, with the family's yes. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: VA:Pr4.1.4a, a written item: one old and one new technology and what each changed for keeping or showing art; VA:Pr5.1.4a, a kept plan card: the work planned for two places, with what protects it in each, naming indoor or outdoor, short or long, physical or digital; VA:Pr6.1.4a, a written item: what a museum, a gallery and a community center each aim to do and what a visitor gets from each; VA:Cn11.1.4a, a written item: three clues on an unseen object card and an inference of when, where and in what culture it was made; VA:Cn10.1.4a, a guide look-for on the tradition piece: the tradition shown, and its community source named on the label. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: the curator's role (grade 5). Tools: setting cards, the two-places plan card, object clue cards, venue cards, Curio Class's own; a slide set on the teacher screen or its printed version. Free path: printed slides on paper, cereal-box mounts. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | Last in year 4 because the show is built from the year's kept work and its own tradition pieces, after the year-end check (week 25); the unit's showing in weeks 26 to 32 is its own assessment. Five ids, so it has 14 days and two check days. Inference from clues builds on unit 7's background cards | Candidate: the community source list checked open and free for the school's own town on the day the unit plan is written | Community tradition: no child is asked to explain their own family's culture or religion; a public or invented tradition is marked the same; a tradition is shown only from a source the community offers, and a sacred or ceremonial practice is never made or imitated. Mogao's caves are Buddhist temples: studied by looking only, no child copies their figures; the sensitivity agent reads the World Heritage listing and the Dunhuang Academy's own pages first. DinAc weaving: the agent reads the holding museum's page and the weaver's own published words first; the unit uses no sandpainting or Yei designs, and no child copies a DinAc design. Family yes for showing outside class. Glue sticks; hands washed. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 9 | 5 | Many readings. *Why do people read the same picture in different ways, and what cultural links can an image carry?* | 16 | va-g5-look; va-g5-craft | VA:Re7.1.5a; VA:Re7.2.5a; VA:Cr2.1.5a; VA:Cr2.2.5a | Responding (Re7); creating (Cr2). Works: Diego Rivera, The Flower Carrier (Mexico, 1935, the San Francisco Museum of Modern Art), described and linked, a picture read as beauty by some and as heavy work by others; a New Year print from Yangliuqing (Tianjin, China, a print tradition with links of luck and plenty such as a child holding a fish; the unit plan picks a print on an open-access museum page); Jaune Quick-to-See Smith, State Names (2000, the Smithsonian American Art Museum; a citizen of the Confederated Salish and Kootenai Nation; the unit plan records her dates), a map of the United States read for the Native names of states, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: reactions before and after, and parts that carry a message (va-g4-look); invented techniques and the tool check (va-g4-craft). VA:Re7.1.5a: write a reading of a work, compare it with a classmate's reading and with a Curio Class reading card, and say where they agree and differ, each with evidence. VA:Re7.2.5a: find and analyze the cultural links an image suggests (a fish for plenty, a flower market, a place name), with the clue each comes from. VA:Cr2.1.5a: build skill in several techniques by experiment and practice logged in the sketchbook: watercolor washes (flat, graded, wet into wet), color mixing, and oil pastel scratch-through, with dated entries. VA:Cr2.2.5a: show fine craft by caring for and using supplies, tools and equipment well (brushes washed and laid flat, palettes cleaned, paper edges kept clean, tools returned). Words: reading, evidence, cultural link, wash, technique, craft. Showing: the logged practice pages beside one finished piece, for the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Re7.1.5a, a written item: the child's reading of an unseen work compared with a given reading card, one agreement and one difference with evidence; VA:Re7.2.5a, a written item: two cultural links an unseen image suggests, with the clue for each; VA:Cr2.1.5a, a guide look-for on the practice log: three techniques over at least three dated entries each, with what improved; VA:Cr2.2.5a, a table item and a look-for: each child at the table cleans and stores a brush and palette by the craft card, and the finished piece shows care (clean edges, clean color). Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: environmental cost of supplies (grade 6). Tools: a color-mixing practice tool or its printed mixing strip, reading cards, the craft card, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: watercolor from a shared tin or watered paint, crayon for scratch-through. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in year 5 because it reopens the studio with care as craft and the practice log, and turns grade 4's named parts into readings compared and culture as a clue. 16 days: the studio routine is known by grade 5, so unit 10 can have the days its written statement needs | Candidate: a fourth image with cultural links from Africa, described and linked | Readings of a work differ: no reading is marked wrong for its view, only for missing evidence; a child is never asked to speak for their own culture. Quick-to-See Smith's map names Native nations whose lands became states: the unit says so plainly and the sensitivity agent reads the museum's page and the artist's own published words first. Rivera's flower vendor carries a heavy load: the agent reads SFMOMA's page first. Hazards and controls: watercolor and oil pastel labeled non-toxic; water cups half full, spills wiped at once; scratch-through with a wooden stick or paper clip, never a blade; hands washed. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 10 | 5 | Two ideas, one new work. *How do artists research and merge ideas into something new, and how does an artist statement explain the choices?* | 18 | va-g5-ideas; va-g5-revise | VA:Cr1.1.5a; VA:Cr1.2.5a; VA:Cr3.1.5a | Creating (Cr1, Cr3); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: the Soundsuits of Nick Cave (United States, born 1959; wearable sculptures that join a costume and a sculpture; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked; masks made from cut plastic fuel cans by Romuald HazoumA" (Porto-Novo, Benin, born 1962; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked; pages from Leonardo da Vinci's notebooks (Italy, about 1480 to 1519, drawings that research flight, water and plants; the Royal Collection Trust's pages, public domain images whose terms the unit plan checks) | Retrieval in day 2's opener: several approaches and a group goal (va-g4-ideas) and the critique protocol (va-g4-revise). VA:Cr1.1.5a: merge two ideas (an animal and a machine, a garment and a building) into a fresh idea for a relief or soft sculpture. VA:Cr1.2.5a: show and name more than one way of researching art (looking at works, interviewing a classmate or a school adult, sketching from objects, reading a short card), then choose one to begin and say why. VA:Cr3.1.5a: write an artist statement that uses art words to explain two choices, then revise the work and the statement after the critique. The artist-statement model starts. Technique: mixed-media relief or soft sculpture from cast-offs (card, fabric scraps, yarn, buttons, foil), joined by tabs, glue and sewing with a blunt needle. Words: merge, research, method, interview, artist statement, revise. Showing: the merged sculptures with their statements, for another class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Cr1.1.5a, a guide look-for on the sketchbook: two ideas named and the merged sketch that joins them; VA:Cr1.2.5a, a kept card made on the last making day: two research methods shown on the pages, the one chosen and why; VA:Cr3.1.5a, a guide look-for on the revised statement: three art words explaining two choices, and the change made after the critique. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a planned inquiry with sources (grade 6). Tools: research-method cards, the statement frame card, the critique-protocol card, Curio Class's own. Free path: cast-offs, tape, yarn, scrap fabric. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | After unit 9 because the sculpture uses the logged techniques and craft care; longest year 5 unit (18 days) because it drafts, critiques and revises two products, a work and a written statement. Before unit 11 because labeling places in art words and comparing standards need the statement's art words | Candidate: a merged-idea work by a living maker from Asia or Latin America, described and linked | Interviews: of a classmate or a school adult only, or an invented person from a card, marked the same; no family or personal facts are written down. HazoumA"'s masks come from fuel-can trade in Benin: the unit tells the reuse and the setting, the masks are his own art, not ceremonial masks, and the sensitivity agent reads the museum's page and the artist's own published words first; no child wears a mask. Hazards and controls: cast-offs cleaned, no glass, no sharp metal, no food containers with allergen traces; blunt yarn needles counted out and in; any cardboard cut with a blade is cut by an adult; no hot glue. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 11 | 5 | Places that matter, eyes that judge. *How can art and design words help us see our own places freshly, and why have people judged art by different standards?* | 14 | va-g5-design; va-g5-judge | VA:Cr2.3.5a; VA:Cn10.1.5a; VA:Re8.1.5a; VA:Re9.1.5a | Creating (Cr2); connecting (Cn10); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: Fan Kuan, Travelers among Mountains and Streams (China, about 1000, a hanging scroll at the National Palace Museum, Taipei), described and linked, judged by its painters for brush and spirit; Berthe Morisot, The Cradle (France, 1872, the MusAce d'Orsay; public domain, the unit plan checks the image's terms), called unfinished by the judges of its day; a tile panel from Iznik (Ottoman Turkey, about 1560 to 1580, the Metropolitan Museum of Art, open access; the unit plan picks the panel), a pattern built on symmetry | Retrieval in day 2's opener: built places recorded from looking (va-g4-design) and one checklist on two works (va-g4-judge); a second retrieval task on day 3 for lines of symmetry (math grade 4, Cross-subject). VA:Cr2.3.5a: name, describe and record in pictures the places or objects that matter to the child (a map or a set of sketches), with a public or invented place marked the same. VA:Cn10.1.5a: label the map or sketches with art and design words (line, pattern, symmetry, balance, value, emphasis) and use the labels to make one work that shows something in the surroundings never noticed before. VA:Re8.1.5a: find a work's ideas and mood by studying its form and structure, its setting, its subject, its visual elements and its media. VA:Re9.1.5a: describe how the standards people used to judge art differ with style, kind of art, media, time and culture, on the three works' setting cards. Words: symmetry, pattern, structure, visual element, style, standard. Showing: the "seeing it freshly" works with their labeled maps, for families or another class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: VA:Cr2.3.5a, a guide look-for on the map or sketch set: places or objects that matter named, described and drawn; VA:Cn10.1.5a, a guide look-for on the made work: at least four art and design words used as labels, and one thing never noticed before shown; VA:Re8.1.5a, a written item: the ideas and mood of an unseen work from its form, structure, setting card, subject, visual elements and media; VA:Re9.1.5a, a written item: two unseen works from different styles or cultures, each with a setting card, and one way the standards used to judge each differ. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: designing for named users (grade 6). Tools: the "made for" card, a symmetry fold card, setting cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencils, scrap paper, a folded paper square. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 10 because labeling in art and design words and comparing standards use the statement's art words; before unit 12 because a curator's argument and an exhibit's evidence need standards and settings already compared | Candidate: a fourth work judged by different standards, from Africa or the Americas, described and linked | Places that matter: invited, never required, with a public or invented place marked the same; no home address or route is drawn. Iznik tiles were made for mosques, palaces and tombs: the unit tells the setting, studies the pattern by looking, and the sensitivity agent reads the Met's page first. Hazards and controls: pencils and colored pencils only; the walk to look for pattern and symmetry stays on school grounds. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 12 | 5 | Curators and change. *What does a curator do, how does an exhibit teach about a topic, and how can art change what people believe or do?* | 12 | va-g5-show; va-g5-museum | VA:Pr4.1.5a; VA:Pr5.1.5a; VA:Pr6.1.5a; VA:Cn11.1.5a | Presenting (Pr4, Pr5, Pr6); connecting (Cn11). Works: the exhibition Nation to Nation: Treaties Between the United States and American Indian Nations (the Smithsonian's National Museum of the American Indian, Washington, opened 2014; its own online pages), studied for how objects, labels and layout present a topic; Lewis Hine's photographs of child workers (United States, 1908 to 1924, the Library of Congress, no known restrictions), photographs that helped change laws; Maya Lin, the Vietnam Veterans Memorial (Washington, 1982; the artist born 1959; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked, a memorial that changed how people remember | Retrieval in day 2's opener: a plan for two places and formats (va-g4-show) and setting inferred from clues (va-g4-museum). VA:Pr4.1.5a: define a curator's roles and responsibilities and the skills and knowledge needed to preserve, maintain and present objects, artifacts and artwork (handling with clean hands, light and damp, condition notes, labels). VA:Pr5.1.5a: argue for safe, effective materials and techniques for preparing and showing the class's work (mounts, photo corners or glue stick against tape and pins), with two reasons and one risk avoided. VA:Pr6.1.5a: cite evidence from the exhibition's pages (an object, a label, the order of rooms) of how it presents ideas and information on its topic. VA:Cn11.1.5a: describe how art can change what a person or a society believes, values or does (Hine's photographs and child labor laws; Lin's memorial and the names on it). Words: curator, preserve, evidence, exhibit, argue, change. Showing: the year-end curated show of the year's work, with the artist statements, for families; children take the curator's jobs, with the family's yes for showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Pr4.1.5a, a written item: three curator jobs (preserve, maintain, present) and the skill or knowledge each needs; VA:Pr5.1.5a, a kept argument card made on the last making day: the chosen materials and mounting, two reasons and one risk avoided; VA:Pr6.1.5a, a written item: two pieces of evidence from a printed exhibit page set the class has not studied, and how each presents the topic; VA:Cn11.1.5a, a written item: how one of the works changed what people believed, valued or did. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: judging a museum's view of its community (grade 6). Tools: curator job cards, the argument card, printed exhibit page sets, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: cereal-box mounts, photo corners cut from scrap card. Once-a-week path: 5 sessions | Last in the band because the curated show is built from the year's kept work and statements and ends the year after the year-end check (week 26); the unit's showing in weeks 27 to 32 is its own assessment. The museum thread then goes to grade 6's judged museum | Candidate: a second exhibit's pages from outside the United States for the unseen evidence item | Hard history: the treaties exhibit tells of agreements made and broken; the unit says so plainly, studies how the exhibit is built, and has no role play; the sensitivity agent reads the museum's own pages first, written with the Native nations. Hine's photographs show children at hard and dangerous work: the unit picks photographs without injury, and the agent reads the Library of Congress pages first. The memorial lists the names of the dead: the unit tells its design and its visitors, not the war's battles, and a child with family loss may take the Hine photographs instead. Health's steps if a child tells something. Glue sticks; hands washed. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |

## Course-level checks

| Check | After unit | Check days | Respond days | What it checks |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Year 3 mid-year (week 18, days 35 and 36) | 2 | 1 | 1 | Samples units 1 and 2 in their own year: a written art-detective sheet on an unseen print or collage, two clues to how it was made and the message it sends (VA:Re7.1.3a, VA:Re7.2.3a), and a fresh short task adding two layers of detail and depth to a given invented-creature sketch (VA:Cr1.1.3a). Unit 2's revised work and note (its check day, day 33) are carried unscored; no idea-page task repeats. Respond on day 36. |
| Year 3 year-end (week 26, days 51 and 52) | 3 | 1 | 1 | Samples units 1 to 3: a written response on an unseen work, how its media build subject and mood and what it means (VA:Re8.1.3a), the same work judged with a new three-line checklist (VA:Re9.1.3a), and a given drawing revised by one added detail with a one-line reason (VA:Cr3.1.3a, unit 2). Unit 3's product records (days 48 and 49) are carried unscored. Unit 4 is assessed by its own showing. Respond on day 52. |
| Year 4 mid-year (week 17, days 97 and 98) | 6 | 1 | 1 | Samples units 5 and 6: a written item marking the parts of an unseen poster that carry its message (VA:Re7.2.4a), and three approaches sketched for a new small design problem (VA:Cr1.1.4a). Unit 6's revised section and recorder's sheet (day 95) are carried unscored. Respond on day 98. |
| Year 4 year-end (week 25, days 113 and 114) | 7 | 1 | 1 | Samples units 5 to 7: a written item on an unseen work with its background card (VA:Re8.1.4a), one checklist used on two unseen works (VA:Re9.1.4a), and a given small drawing revised from a given classmate comment, with the change named (VA:Cr3.1.4a, unit 6). Unit 7's product record (day 111) is carried unscored. Unit 8 is assessed by its own showing. Respond on day 114. |
| Year 5 mid-year (week 18, days 163 and 164) | 10 | 1 | 1 | Samples units 9 and 10: a written reading of an unseen work compared with a given reading card (VA:Re7.1.5a), two cultural links in an unseen image with their clues (VA:Re7.2.5a), and a short artist statement in art words for one kept piece of unit 9 (VA:Cr3.1.5a). Unit 10's revised statement (day 161) is carried unscored. Respond on day 164. |
| Year 5 year-end (week 26, days 179 and 180) | 11 | 1 | 1 | Samples units 9 to 11: a written item on the ideas and mood of an unseen work from its form, structure, setting card, subject, visual elements and media (VA:Re8.1.5a), two unseen works from different cultures with setting cards and how their judging standards differ (VA:Re9.1.5a), and two given ideas merged in a quick sketch (VA:Cr1.1.5a, unit 10). Unit 11's product records (days 176 and 177) are carried unscored. Unit 12 is assessed by its own showing. Respond on day 180. |

Each check day runs as the brief's check day: set-up, a self-check, a written sitting on unseen works read aloud on
request, the teacher's table for any child the teacher writes for, and clean-up, in 40 minutes with no paint or clay.
Every child's evidence is collected once in a window; no artifact is scored twice. Days above are band course days
(year 4 starts on day 65, year 5 on day 129).

## Cross-subject

Two visual arts rows of grades 3 to 5 carry a cross_subject lean (data/progressions/arts.csv); both are core courses
(lesson 32). The other contacts are practiced, not owned, each with its "if later" line.

### English language arts, grade 3 (same year)

| Used here | Lean | Where ELA teaches it | If ELA has not taught it yet |
|---|---|---|---|
| Unit 2 (year 3, course days 19 to 34, school weeks 10 to 17): the partner talk before a detail is added (va-g3-revise) | ela:SL.3.1, taking part in a collaborative talk with a partner | English language arts grade 3 unit 1 (days 1 to 39, weeks 1 to 8) | The unit's partner talk card shows the turn-taking rules (one speaks, one listens, ask one question) and the teacher models them on the first talk day |

The written artist notes and statements (units 4 and 10) and the written items practice ELA grades 3 to 5 writing;
practiced, not owned.

### Mathematics, grade 4 (the year before year 5)

| Used here | Lean | Where math teaches it | If a child missed it |
|---|---|---|---|
| Unit 11 (year 5): lines of symmetry in a design and in the surroundings (va-g5-design) | math:4.G.A.3, finding lines of symmetry in a design | Math grade 4 unit 9 (days 128 to 149), one grade back | Day 3's retrieval task carries a ten-minute reteach: fold a paper shape so the halves match, and mark the fold as the line of symmetry; no measuring is asked |

No unit asks for measuring, ratio, fractions or scale (lesson 13): unit 3's maps keep size order only, and unit 7's
horizon line is a drawn line, not a measured one.

### Social studies, grades 3 to 5

Unit 3's maps (year 3), unit 4's histories, unit 8's community traditions (year 4) and unit 12's treaties and child
labor (year 5) sit near social studies topics of the same grades. No lean either way: the art units teach the map
words and the settings they use themselves, on setting and work cards (bar criterion 7).

## For the lead

1. **After-core check weeks.** This frame's checks (years 3 and 5 weeks 18 and 26, year 4 weeks 17 and 25) stay off
   every core check week of their grades. `frame.py calendar` shows four after-core clashes, all with
   physical-education-grades-3-to-5: grade 3 weeks 18 and 26, grade 5 weeks 18 and 26. Weeks not moved, by the
   job's rule; left for the lead's one calendar pass. Year 4 (weeks 17 and 25) shows no clash today.
2. **Words-file lines shorter than the national actions.** VA:Pr5.1.4a (line 1096) reads "different places, times,
   and formats"; the national standard names indoor or outdoor, temporary or permanent, physical or digital.
   VA:Re8.1.5a (line 1145) leaves out visual elements. The frame teaches both in full (units 8 and 11). Pick: keep
   the lines (14-word limit), since the units name the parts; or replace VA:Pr5.1.4a with "Analyze showing and
   guarding art indoors or out, short or long term, physical or digital."
3. **Row spelling.** va-g5-design's notes say "labelled"; US spelling is "labeled" (Row changes below).
4. **Supplies ceiling (money, for Cory).** Provisional costs: $60 (year 3), $50 (year 4), $50 (year 5) a class of 30,
   all optional beside a free path. Pick: the same $75 a class a year as the K to 2 frame's pick, with every unit's
   free path kept.
5. **Facts the unit plans confirm at source.** Several works are named from memory and not opened for this frame
   (no downloads or page reads were made): Bearden's The Block at the Met, Catlett's Sharecropper at the Art
   Institute of Chicago, Jaune Quick-to-See Smith's State Names at the Smithsonian American Art Museum and her
   dates, D.Y. Begay's holding page, El-Salahi's holding page, the Chicano Park start date, the Nation to Nation
   pages and the Iznik panel. Each unit plan opens the item's own page for the rights line, as the Base text
   bullet says; a work whose page fails is swapped with the unit's ids and the year's range kept.
