261: | 1 | 3 | Art detectives in the studio. *How can clues in a work tell us how it was made and what it says, and how can printing, collage and clay come together in one work?* | 18 | va-g3-look; va-g3-craft | VA:Re7.1.3a; VA:Re7.2.3a; VA:Cr2.1.3a; VA:Cr2.2.3a | Responding (Re7); creating (Cr2); connecting touched through each work's setting card. Works: Romare Bearden, The Block (United States, 1971, a six-panel collage of a Harlem street, the Metropolitan Museum of Art), described and linked, never copied; an Asante adinkra cloth (Ghana, stamped with carved calabash stamps; the unit plan picks a cloth from a museum's open-access page, such as the Smithsonian National Museum of African Art's), studied for how its stamps were made and what its symbols mean; Elizabeth Catlett, Sharecropper (a linocut made in Mexico, 1952, an impression at the Art Institute of Chicago), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: sorting pictures by feeling (va-g2-look) and the clean-up card (va-g2-craft). VA:Re7.1.3a: find clues in a work (edges, layers, repeated marks, tool marks) and guess how the artist made it, then check the guess on the work card. VA:Re7.2.3a: work out the message a picture sends, with a "because" pointing to the detail that sends it. VA:Cr2.1.3a: make a finished work the child is happy with that joins at least two processes: a foam-plate print (lines pressed with a pencil, water-based ink rolled with a brayer), a torn and cut collage layer, and a small modeled clay or dough piece mounted beside it. VA:Cr2.2.3a: show safe, skilled handling in each process (the brayer and ink tray, scissors and glue, the clay tools and wet clay kept covered), aloud and in action. Words: clue, process, print, collage, modeling, message. Showing: the finished panels as a gallery for a partner class, each with a one-line process label. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Re7.1.3a, a written item: two clues on an unseen print or collage and a guess at how it was made; VA:Re7.2.3a, a written item: the message of the same unseen work, with the detail that sends it; VA:Cr2.1.3a, a guide look-for on the finished panel: two or more processes joined, with the child's one-line note on why they are happy with it; VA:Cr2.2.3a, a table item: with five at the table, each child shows the safe, skilled use of one tool from the tray (brayer, scissors or clay tool) and says one rule. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: inventing a technique of one's own (grade 4). Tools: step cards for printing, collage and modeling, a clue card, work cards, Curio Class's own; a work viewer on the teacher screen or the printed card. Free path: foam trays from packaging, washable paint for ink, magazine paper, flour-salt dough. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | First because every later unit of the year needs the studio routine and the looking routine at grade 3's step (the safe-studio and look-describe-interpret models come back here, retrieved where first needed). Guessing a process from clues comes before the child's own printing and collage, so the looking teaches what to try. Longest grade 3 unit: four ids, three processes in one finished work and the year's studio set-up | Candidate: the bar's criterion 9 on set-up and clean-up minutes in a 40-minute printing session with 30 children | Hazards and controls: printing ink and paint on hands and tables (smocks or old shirts, covered tables, hands washed); brayers and ink trays handed out by monitors, never shared mid-roll; blunt-tip scissors only in year 3; clay kept moist, not eaten, wheat allergy checked (moist clay instead of flour dough); foam trays washed, no meat trays. Adinkra cloth was first a mourning cloth: the unit tells it plainly, studies the symbols by looking, and no child copies adinkra symbols (each child designs their own stamp mark); the sensitivity agent reads the holding museum's page and an Asante or Ghanaian source on adinkra first. Catlett's print shows a Black woman farm worker; the unit tells its setting (Catlett's prints of working people, made in Mexico) and the sensitivity agent reads the museum's page first. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
262: | 2 | 3 | Growing an idea. *How do artists make an invented idea richer, and how does a partner's question help us add what a work needs?* | 16 | va-g3-ideas; va-g3-revise | VA:Cr1.1.3a; VA:Cr1.2.3a; VA:Cr3.1.3a | Creating (Cr1, Cr3); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Henri Rousseau, The Repast of the Lion (France, about 1907, the Metropolitan Museum of Art, open access), a jungle he never saw, built from imagination and from the plants and pictures he studied; a page from the Hamzanama (Mughal India, about 1562 to 1577, painted in the emperor's workshop for a long adventure story; the unit plan picks a page without battle from the Brooklyn Museum's or the Metropolitan Museum of Art's open-access pages); the invented worlds of Shaun Tan (Australia, born 1974; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked to the artist's own page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: many solutions and a question of the child's own (va-g2-ideas) and the two-way talk (va-g2-revise). VA:Cr1.1.3a: take a made-up idea (an invented creature, place or machine) and add detail and depth over three idea pages: a foreground, a middle and a background, and details of texture and pattern. VA:Cr1.2.3a: choose and use the resources, tools and technology on hand to pursue the idea (a mirror for a face, a magnifier for texture, a viewfinder, reference picture cards, or a drawing tool on a shared device in turns) and say what each helped with. VA:Cr3.1.3a: after a short partner talk (describe, ask, suggest), add detail that strengthens the meaning that is appearing, and write one line on what was added and why. Technique: overlapping for depth, and layering colored pencil or oil pastel. Words: imagine, detail, depth, foreground, background, strengthen. Showing: the invented worlds with their idea pages beside them, for the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Cr1.1.3a, a guide look-for on the idea pages: the first idea and at least two added layers of detail or depth; VA:Cr1.2.3a, a kept card made on the last making day: the two tools or resources the child chose and what each helped them do; VA:Cr3.1.3a, a guide look-for on the revised work with its one-line note: a detail added after the partner talk that makes the meaning clearer. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: brainstorming routes with a group (grade 4). Tools: idea-page step cards, the "tools I chose" card, a partner talk card, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencils, scrap paper, a hand mirror. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 1 because adding depth uses the clue-reading of how works are layered, and the year's studio is set; before unit 3 because a map and an observed drawing need the habit of adding detail and the partner talk that decides what to add. The partner talk practices ELA grade 3's collaborative talk (Cross-subject) | Candidate: a fourth invented world by a living maker from Africa or Latin America, described and linked | Hamzanama pages often show battles and demons: the unit plan picks a page without violence, and the sensitivity agent reads the museum's page first. Partner talk: a child who finds talk hard may point and use the talk card's frames. Oil pastel and colored pencil labeled non-toxic; hands washed. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
263: | 3 | 3 | Maps, models and mood. *How can a map or model show a place we know, and how do an artist's materials make a mood we can judge?* | 14 | va-g3-design; va-g3-judge | VA:Cr2.3.3a; VA:Cn10.1.3a; VA:Re8.1.3a; VA:Re9.1.3a | Creating (Cr2); connecting (Cn10); responding (Re8, Re9). Works: a stick chart from the Marshall Islands (Micronesia, nineteenth or twentieth century, sticks and shells mapping ocean swells and islands for navigators; the unit plan picks one on a holding museum's page), described and linked; Gustave Caillebotte, Paris Street; Rainy Day (France, 1877, the Art Institute of Chicago, open access), a built place and a mood made with paint | Retrieval in day 2's opener: an old object given a new use (va-g2-design) and meaning from mood, subject and form (va-g2-judge). VA:Cr2.3.3a: alone or in a group, make a map, diagram or model of a familiar place (the classroom, the playground or the route to the library), with a key of three symbols, and a box-and-paper model of one part. VA:Cn10.1.3a: make a drawing from what the child observes on a walk around the school, with three details that are really there. VA:Re8.1.3a: work out what a work means by studying how its media (paint, sticks, ink) build its subject, its form and its mood. VA:Re9.1.3a: judge a work with the teacher's three-line checklist, one reason for each line (the standards-set model begins). Words: map, key, symbol, model, mood, checklist. Showing: the maps and models as a "know our school" table for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: VA:Cr2.3.3a, a guide look-for on the map or model: the place recognizable, a key with three symbols, parts in the order they really sit; VA:Cn10.1.3a, a guide look-for on the observed drawing: three details seen on the walk; VA:Re8.1.3a, a written item: how the media of an unseen work build its subject and mood, and what it means; VA:Re9.1.3a, a written item: the same work judged with a new three-line checklist, a reason for each line. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: measured scale (bigger things are drawn bigger, in the order they sit; no ratio or measuring, lesson 13); built places of the region (grade 4). Tools: the "made for" card, map step cards, the checklist card, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: cereal boxes, scrap paper, pencils. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 2 because a map and an observed drawing build on adding detail; judging with a checklist needs unit 1's clue-reading and adds media and mood. Before unit 4 so the year's show has maps, models and invented worlds to choose from. The standards-set model starts here | Candidate: a third place-and-mood work by a living maker from Asia or Africa, described and linked | Hazards and controls: the school walk stays on school grounds, the class together and in sight; cardboard cut with a blade is cut by an adult; glue sticks and tape. Maps show the school only, never a child's home or route home. Stick charts carry navigators' knowledge: the sensitivity agent reads the holding museum's page and a Marshallese source on navigation first; no child is asked to copy one. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
264: | 4 | 3 | Stories and history in art. *How have people used art to tell stories and keep history, and how does knowing when and where a work was made change what we think of it?* | 12 | va-g3-show; va-g3-museum | VA:Pr4.1.3a; VA:Pr5.1.3a; VA:Pr6.1.3a; VA:Cn11.1.3a | Presenting (Pr4, Pr5, Pr6); connecting (Cn11); responding practiced on each work (review). Works: Jacob Lawrence, The Migration Series (United States, 1940 to 1941, 60 panels shared by the Phillips Collection and the Museum of Modern Art), chosen panels described and linked; a Lakota winter count (pictures painted on hide or cloth, one for each year, keeping a band's history; the unit plan picks one on the Smithsonian's pages), described and linked; the Bayeux embroidery (Normandy, about 1070, wool on linen telling the Norman conquest of England, the Bayeux Museum), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: a theme exhibit (va-g2-show) and two cultures on work cards (va-g2-museum). VA:Pr4.1.3a: walk the classroom, the hallway and a paper mock-up of the class web page as show spaces, and list what each can and cannot do for a work. VA:Pr5.1.3a: choose one space, mount the work and write a short artist note (title, process, one sentence of meaning). VA:Pr6.1.3a: describe how and where three cultures told stories and kept history in art (panels in a series, a picture for each year, a long embroidery). VA:Cn11.1.3a: write a first reaction to a work, read its setting card, and say how the reaction changed. Words: story, history, record, setting, space, artist note. Showing: the year-end class show in the chosen spaces, for families, with the family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Pr4.1.3a, a kept card finished on the unit's last making day: the three spaces, one "can" and one "cannot" each; VA:Pr5.1.3a, a guide look-for on the showing: the chosen space, the mounted work and its artist note; VA:Pr6.1.3a, a written item: how and where one of the three cultures recorded a story or history; VA:Cn11.1.3a, a written item: a first reaction to an unseen work, then the reaction after its setting card, and what changed. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: technology for keeping art (grade 4). Tools: the show-space card, artist-note card, work and setting cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: cereal-box mounts. Once-a-week path: 5 sessions | Last in year 3 because the show is chosen from the year's kept work (units 1 to 3) and ends the year after the year-end check (week 26); the unit's showing in weeks 27 to 32 is its own assessment. The exhibit-plan and work-card models move to grade 3's step here | Candidate: a story-telling work from Asia or Africa beside the three, by a living maker | Hard history: the Great Migration was a flight from violence and unfair laws; the unit picks panels of travel, work and new homes, and leaves out the panels that show violence; the winter count may record war, sickness or hunger years, named in one plain sentence; the Bayeux embroidery shows a battle, shown in the panels of building, sailing and feasting. The sensitivity agent reads the Phillips Collection's pages on the series, the Smithsonian's pages on winter counts with Lakota keepers' own words, and the Bayeux Museum's page first. The class web page is the school's own and only the teacher posts to it, under each child's chosen name with the family's yes. Glue sticks; hands washed. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |

