265: | 5 | 4 | Marks and messages. *How does making a piece like an artist's change what we see in theirs, and which parts of a picture carry its message?* | 16 | va-g4-look; va-g4-craft | VA:Re7.1.4a; VA:Re7.2.4a; VA:Cr2.1.4a; VA:Cr2.2.4a | Responding (Re7); creating (Cr2). Works: Vincent van Gogh, Wheat Field with Cypresses (the Netherlands-born artist in France, 1889, the Metropolitan Museum of Art, open access), marks that move; an ink drawing by Ibrahim El-Salahi (born 1930 in Sudan; the unit plan picks a work on the Tate's or another museum's page and records whether living, as of its date), described and linked; a poster from the Library of Congress's collection of posters made by the national works program of 1936 to 1943 (public domain; the unit plan picks a parks or library poster), a picture built to send one message | Retrieval in day 2's opener: clues to how a work was made (va-g3-look) and safe, skilled handling (va-g3-craft). VA:Re7.1.4a: write a first reaction to a work, make a small piece in similar media (marks in ink or watered paint), write a second reaction, and compare the two. VA:Re7.2.4a: mark the parts of an image that deliver its message (the biggest shape, the words, the colors, where the eye goes first) and say what each part does. VA:Cr2.1.4a: test a set of mark-making tools on a sample sheet (pencil, brush, stick, sponge, finger) and invent and name one technique of the child's own, with its steps. VA:Cr2.2.4a: handle supplies, tools and equipment with care so no one gets hurt, and pass the tool check before using pointed scissors or other pointed tools (carry, use, pass and store, and why each step keeps others safe). The practice-log model starts: each technique is a dated entry in the sketchbook. Words: mark, technique, invent, reaction, message, care. Showing: the sample sheets and the invented-technique cards as a "technique library" for the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Re7.1.4a, a kept card made on the last making days: the first reaction, the small piece, the second reaction and the change named; VA:Re7.2.4a, a written item: two parts of an unseen poster that carry its message and what each does; VA:Cr2.1.4a, a guide look-for on the sample sheet: five tools tested and one invented technique named with its steps; VA:Cr2.2.4a, a table item: each child at the table shows the safe carry, use, pass and storage of pointed scissors and says how it keeps others from harm. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: skill built over a long practice log (grade 5). Tools: a mark sampler tool or its printed sample sheet, step cards, the tool-check card, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: sticks, sponges, watered paint, scrap paper. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in year 4 because it reopens the studio with the tool check before any pointed tool and turns grade 3's clues into named parts that carry a message. Shorter than year 3's unit 1 (16 days): one technique strand, not three processes, so year 4's museum unit can have the days its five ids need. The practice-log model starts here, where it is first needed | Candidate: a mark-making work by a living Native American maker beside the three | Hazards and controls: ink is washable or watered paint (no permanent ink); smocks, covered tables, hands washed; pointed scissors only after the child passes the tool check, carried closed and point down, stored in a closed box; sticks blunt. The national works program poster is government art: the unit tells its setting (jobs for artists in the 1930s). El-Salahi's work draws on Arabic script and Sudanese settings: the sensitivity agent reads the museum's page and the artist's own published words first, and no child copies script as decoration. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
266: | 6 | 4 | Making it together. *How does a group of artists set a goal that matters to them, and how does a critique help a work in progress?* | 16 | va-g4-ideas; va-g4-revise | VA:Cr1.1.4a; VA:Cr1.2.4a; VA:Cr3.1.4a | Creating (Cr1, Cr3); responding practiced on the murals (review). Works: Judy Baca and the young people who painted The Great Wall of Los Angeles (United States, begun 1976, a half-mile mural of California history in a flood channel; the artist's center's own pages; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked; the murals of Chicano Park, San Diego (begun 1973 by community artists under a freeway, on the park's own pages), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: adding detail and depth (va-g3-ideas) and the partner talk (va-g3-revise). VA:Cr1.1.4a: brainstorm at least three different approaches to one design problem (a panel that welcomes new students to the school) before the group chooses. VA:Cr1.2.4a: set goals as a group (what the panel must say, who it is for, each member's part) and make a work that matters to its makers. VA:Cr3.1.4a: change the panel in progress using ideas from a small-group critique with set steps and roles (speaker, listener, recorder; describe, ask, suggest; the artist answers with one planned change). The critique-protocol model starts. Technique: a group paper mural panel with a shared color plan and a grid to join each member's section. Words: brainstorm, approach, goal, group, critique, revise. Showing: the welcome panels hung in the entrance hall for the school. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Cr1.1.4a, a guide look-for on the sketchbook: three approaches that differ, sketched by the child; VA:Cr1.2.4a, a kept card: the group's goal card (goal, why it matters to us, each member's part) and a look-for on the panel: the child's section meets the goal; VA:Cr3.1.4a, a guide look-for on the revised section with the recorder's sheet: one change made from the critique, with the comment it came from. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet; each child is marked on their own sketches, card line and section. Out: merging two ideas (grade 5). Tools: the brainstorm sheet, the goal card, the critique-protocol card with roles, Curio Class's own. Free path: kraft or newsprint roll, two or three colors of paint or crayon. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 5 because the panel uses the year's tested and invented marks and the tool care; the critique-protocol model starts here, before unit 7's judging and every later critique. Before the mid-year check (week 17) so a group product is finished before the check samples it | Candidate: a group-made work from Asia or Africa beside the two murals | Group work: each child's mark rests on their own sketches, their line of the goal card and their own section, never on the group's whole panel. "Matters to its makers" is the school, not a child's home; a child may draw a public or invented welcome scene. The Great Wall of Los Angeles tells hard parts of California history: the unit names its making with young people and shows sections the sensitivity agent passes; Chicano Park's murals grew from a community's claim to a park: the agent reads the park's own pages and SPARC's (the artist's center) first. Hazards and controls: paint on hands and floor (drop cloths, covered tables, hands washed); the roll laid on tables, never on a stair or a walkway. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
