h the family's yes for showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: VA:Pr4.1.5a, a written item: three curator jobs (preserve, maintain, present) and the skill or knowledge each needs; VA:Pr5.1.5a, a kept argument card made on the last making day: the chosen materials and mounting, two reasons and one risk avoided; VA:Pr6.1.5a, a written item: two pieces of evidence from a printed exhibit page set the class has not studied, and how each presents the topic; VA:Cn11.1.5a, a written item: how one of the works changed what people believed, valued or did. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: judging a museum's view of its community (grade 6). Tools: curator job cards, the argument card, printed exhibit page sets, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: cereal-box mounts, photo corners cut from scrap card. Once-a-week path: 5 sessions | Last in the band because the curated show is built from the year's kept work and statements and ends the year after the year-end check (week 26); the unit's showing in weeks 27 to 32 is its own assessment. The museum thread then goes to grade 6's judged museum | Candidate: a second exhibit's pages from outside the United States for the unseen evidence item | Hard history: the treaties exhibit tells of agreements made and broken; the unit says so plainly, studies how the exhibit is built, and has no role play; the sensitivity agent reads the museum's own pages first, written with the Native nations. Hine's photographs show children at hard and dangerous work: the unit picks photographs without injury, and the agent reads the Library of Congress pages first. The memorial lists the names of the dead: the unit tells its design and its visitors, not the war's battles, and a child with family loss may take the Hine photographs instead. Health's steps if a child tells something. Glue sticks; hands washed. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
